HI_P7C_Q = "A literature student is reading a poem set in Berlin in August 1961. For that student, write one paragraph of no more than 200 words outlining the historical context: the scale of emigration from East Germany to the West between 1949 and 1961, the closing of the sector border on 13 August 1961, and the building of the Wall in the weeks that followed, with dated and quantified evidence. (I'm an SL History student sitting my exams in May 2027, on the syllabus first assessed in 2017, and I study Paper 2 topic 12, the Cold War; this is a non-assessed short answer.)"
HI_P7C = "The closure of the sector border on 13 August 1961 ended a drain that had run for more than twelve years, and the Wall that followed was the answer to pressure that had built up over that time, not a sudden act. Between the founding of the German Democratic Republic on 7 October 1949 and 1961, millions of East Germans left for the West, and many of them were young people and skilled workers whom the East German economy could least afford to lose. In 1952 the GDR sealed its border with West Germany, which made the open sector border in Berlin the main route out. In the early hours of 13 August 1961, East German forces closed that route, first with barbed wire. Over the following weeks the barbed wire gave way to concrete, and a wall was built around West Berlin. The closure therefore dealt with the exit rather than the reasons people left, and the flow fell sharply once the route was sealed.\n\nNote: my sources do not confirm the exact emigration total, so the figure is given as 'millions'; check the total in your textbook before quoting it."

HI_T13_Q = "I'm an SL History student sitting my exams in May 2027, so I'm on the syllabus first assessed in 2017. This is for Paper 2, world history topic 10 (Authoritarian states). Since each Paper 2 essay is marked out of 20 and we get a full hour for each one, I want exam depth. Essay question: 'To what extent did Mussolini consolidate his power between 1922 and 1929 through legal rather than violent means?' Write one body paragraph only, following your method, in no more than 350 words, with dated and quantified evidence."
HI_T13 = "Benito Mussolini consolidated his power between 1922 and 1929 largely through legal and constitutional means, but those means worked because they rested on the violence that had brought him to power. Consolidation here means securing power already gained, not seizing it. The electoral route came first. The Acerbo Law of November 1923 gave two-thirds of the seats to the list with the most votes, provided that list won at least 25 per cent. In the election of 6 April 1924, held under that law, the Fascist list won a clear majority of the vote and, with it, the seats the law guaranteed. That election took place in a climate of intimidation. When the Socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti was abducted on 10 June 1924, the opposition deputies withdrew from the Chamber, yet King Victor Emmanuel III did not dismiss Mussolini. The legal route became decisive after that crisis. On 3 January 1925 Mussolini accepted responsibility for the violence before the Chamber, and between December 1925 and November 1926 a series of laws gave the head of government powers beyond parliamentary control, banned opposition parties and created the Special Tribunal for the Defence of the State. Each measure passed through existing state institutions, so each was legal in form. By 1928 a further law had made membership of the Chamber depend on a single list drawn up by the Fascist Grand Council, which voters approved in a plebiscite in March 1929. Yet the laws worked largely because the earlier violence had weakened resistance: the opposition deputies had stayed out of the Chamber since the Matteotti crisis, so the laws met little organised resistance. The question's contrast between legal and violent means therefore overstates the difference. Legal means did most of the consolidating after 1925, but violence had already shaped the terms on which they worked.\n\nNote: Paper 2 essays are marked out of 15, with about 45 minutes each (two essays in 1 hour 30 minutes), not out of 20 with an hour each, so this paragraph follows the IB figures; my sources do not confirm the 1924 vote share, so the quantified evidence is the dates and the Acerbo Law's terms."

EC_P7A_Q = "I'm an HL Economics student (the course first assessed in 2022; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 3 practice, part (a), using the supplied data on Varenland's drinks tax. A Business Management student will use your answer to analyse a drinks producer, so state each result clearly with its units. (i) Calculate the price elasticity of demand for sugar-sweetened drinks between 2024 and 2025. [2] (ii) Calculate the government's tax revenue in 2025. [1] (iii) Calculate the tax burden per litre and in total for consumers and for producers in 2025. [2] (iv) Calculate the change in producers' revenue, after tax, between 2024 and 2025. [2] (v) Using a diagram, explain why consumers bear most of the burden of this tax. [3] Write no more than 350 words, plus working and your diagram."
EC_P7A = "(i) [2 marks]\n\nMeasure: price elasticity of demand (PED) for sugar-sweetened drinks, 2024 to 2025, using the price paid by consumers.\nPercentage change in quantity demanded = (105 − 120) ÷ 120 × 100 = −12.50%\nPercentage change in price = (1.90 − 1.60) ÷ 1.60 × 100 = 18.75%\nPED = −12.50 ÷ 18.75 = −0.67\nResult: PED = −0.67. Demand is price inelastic, because its absolute value, 0.67, is less than 1.\n\n(ii) [1 mark]\n\nTax revenue, 2025 = tax per litre × quantity sold after the tax = $0.40 × 105 million litres = $42 million per year.\nResult: government tax revenue in 2025 was $42 million per year.\n\n(iii) [2 marks]\n\nMeasure: tax burden per litre and in total, 2025, for consumers and for producers.\nPre-tax price, 2024 (no tax): P₁ = $1.60 per litre\nPrice paid by consumers, 2025: Pc = $1.90 per litre\nPrice received by producers, 2025: Pp = $1.90 − $0.40 = $1.50 per litre\nConsumers: ($1.90 − $1.60) = $0.30 per litre; $0.30 × 105 million litres = $31.50 million per year\nProducers: ($1.60 − $1.50) = $0.10 per litre; $0.10 × 105 million litres = $10.50 million per year\nCheck: $31.50 million + $10.50 million = $42.00 million per year, equal to the tax revenue in (ii).\nResult: consumers bear $0.30 per litre, or $31.50 million per year (75% of the tax); producers bear $0.10 per litre, or $10.50 million per year (25% of the tax).\n\n(iv) [2 marks]\n\nMeasure: producers' revenue after tax = price received by producers × quantity sold.\n2024 = $1.60 × 120 million litres = $192.00 million per year (no tax, so the price received equals the price paid)\n2025 = $1.50 × 105 million litres = $157.50 million per year\nChange = $157.50 million − $192.00 million = −$34.50 million per year\nPercentage change = −34.50 ÷ 192.00 × 100 = −17.97%\nCheck: consumer spending in 2025 = $1.90 × 105 million litres = $199.50 million = $157.50 million + $42.00 million tax revenue.\nResult: producers' revenue after tax fell by $34.50 million per year (17.97%) between 2024 and 2025.\n\n(v) [3 marks]\n\nDiagram 1: The market for sugar-sweetened drinks in Varenland, with a $0.40 per litre tax\nAxes: vertical — Price ($ per litre); horizontal — Quantity of sugar-sweetened drinks (million litres per year)\nCurves: D, downward sloping; S₁, upward sloping; S₂, parallel to S₁ and $0.40 per litre above it\nChange: the tax shifts supply up from S₁ to S₂ by $0.40 per litre (arrow from S₁ to S₂)\nEquilibria: before the tax, P₁ = $1.60 at Q₁ = 120 million litres; after it, Pc = $1.90 paid by consumers and Pp = $1.50 received by producers, at Q₂ = 105 million litres\nAreas: the rectangle between P₁ and Pc over Q₂ is the consumers' burden ($0.30 × 105 million litres); the rectangle between Pp and P₁ over Q₂ is the producers' burden ($0.10 × 105 million litres)\n\nConsumers bear most of the tax because demand is relatively price inelastic: a PED of −0.67 means that the quantity bought falls by proportionally less than the price rises (12.50% against 18.75%). In Diagram 1 the tax shifts supply up from S₁ to S₂, and the market settles at 105 million litres, where consumers pay Pc = $1.90 and producers keep Pp = $1.50. Consumers therefore pay $0.30 of the $0.40 tax per litre, and producers absorb only $0.10. The data give demand's elasticity but not supply's, so the claim that demand responds less than supply is inferred from this split rather than measured."

BM_T13_Q = "I'm an SL Business Management student (the guide first assessed in 2024; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 2 practice using the supplied case on Hollin & Daughters. (a) Calculate the monthly break-even quantity and the margin of safety at current output. [4] (b) Calculate the gross profit margin and the profit margin for 2025. [4] (c) Explain one limitation of break-even analysis for Hollin & Daughters. [2] Show your working."
BM_T13 = '(a) [4]\n\nContribution per loaf = selling price − variable cost per loaf\n= $4.00 − ($1.10 + $0.90)\n= $4.00 − $2.00 = $2.00 per loaf\n\nMonthly fixed costs = rent + wages of two bakers + insurance and other fixed costs\n= $2,500 + $2,800 + $700 = $6,000 per month\n\nBreak-even quantity = fixed costs ÷ contribution per loaf\n= $6,000 ÷ $2.00 = 3,000 loaves per month\n\nMargin of safety = current output − break-even quantity\n= 4,200 − 3,000 = 1,200 loaves per month\nAs a percentage of current output: 1,200 ÷ 4,200 × 100 = 28.57%\n\n(b) [4]\n\nCheck first: the 2025 accounts agree with Table 1. 4,200 × 12 = 50,400 loaves; 50,400 × $4.00 = $201,600 of sales revenue; 50,400 × $2.00 = $100,800 of cost of sales; 12 × $6,000 = $72,000 of expenses.\n\nGross profit margin = (gross profit ÷ sales revenue) × 100\n= ($100,800 ÷ $201,600) × 100 = 50.00%\n\nProfit margin = (profit before interest and tax ÷ sales revenue) × 100\n= ($28,800 ÷ $201,600) × 100 = 14.29%\n\nTable 2 gives no interest or tax figures, so profit before interest and tax is the profit used.\n\n(c) [2]\n\nBreak-even analysis assumes a constant selling price and that all output is sold, so the break-even of 3,000 loaves holds only at $4.00 a loaf. Hollin & Daughters sells only to three cafes, and a price concession from them would raise its break-even point. At 4,200 loaves a month, the bakery can absorb a price fall of only about 14.29% (to $3.43 a loaf) before it makes a loss, half the 28.57% volume margin of safety, so the volume figure overstates its protection against a price cut.'

GE_T13_Q = "I'm a Geography SL student (the course first assessed in 2019; my exams are in May 2027). This is a Paper 2 structured question on core unit 1, Changing population, using the supplied data table. (a) Calculate the rate of natural increase, as a percentage, for 1990 and for 2020. [2] (b) Calculate the dependency ratio for 2020. [2] (c) Explain two reasons why the crude birth rate fell between 1990 and 2020. [6] Answer each part in no more than 350 words in total, plus working."
GE_T13 = "(a) Rate of natural increase (%) = (crude birth rate − crude death rate) ÷ 10\nRates are per 1,000, so dividing the difference by 10 gives a percentage.\n\n1990: (31 − 11) ÷ 10\n= 20 ÷ 10\n= 2.0%\n\n2020: (16 − 7) ÷ 10\n= 9 ÷ 10\n= 0.9%\n\nAnswer: the rate of natural increase was 2.0% per year in 1990 and 0.9% per year in 2020.\n\n(b) Population aged 15–64 in 2020 = 100 − 22 − 9 = 69%\nDependency ratio = (aged 0–14 + aged 65 and over) ÷ aged 15–64 × 100\n= (22 + 9) ÷ 69 × 100\n= 31 ÷ 69 × 100\n= 44.9\n\nAnswer: 44.9 dependants per 100 people of working age in 2020. The percentages can be used directly because the total population cancels out. The ratio is below 100, so working-age people outnumber dependants.\n\n(c) Falling mortality helped to lower the birth rate. The crude death rate in Country X fell from 11 to 7 per 1,000 between 1990 and 2020. Better sanitation, food supply and healthcare meant that more infants and children survived, so parents could expect their children to live and needed fewer births to reach the family size they wanted. This helped to lower the crude birth rate, which fell from 31 to 16 per 1,000 over the same period.\n\nRising women's education and urbanisation tend to make children more costly. As more women in Country X gained schooling and paid work, and more families moved to towns where schooling and housing cost more, the opportunity cost of raising children rose. Couples therefore chose smaller families and used contraception more widely, so births fell. The table does not measure education, urbanisation or contraception use, so this reason is a suggested process rather than one the data show."

